  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tackling child labour in coffee supply chains in Vietnam</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Vietnam is the world&#x2019;s largest exporter of Robusta coffee. The fertile Central Highlands are ideal for growing the crop, making it the largest production area for Robusta coffee in Vietnam. Coffee is a leading agricultural export and serves as the main income source for 640,000 households and nearly two million laborers. Smallholder farmers produce 95% of Vietnam&#x2019;s coffee. In 2018, Vietnam had 680,000 ha of land dedicated to coffee cultivation, with an average productivity of 2.5 ton/ha and a yield of 2.62 million tons (Department of Crop Production, MARD).Although the growth of the industry has created thousands of new jobs, many farmers have a very low income, due to the low profitability of the sector. Not all farmers have been able to adapt to the high input requirements and intensive production systems. The International Center for Tropical Agriculture reported that as of 2015, 11.3% of the population in the Central Highlands was living under the national poverty line.The impact assessment conducted in Project A revealed that in both Gia Lai and &#xd0;?k L?k provinces, differences exist in terms of income, quality of life and awareness on children&#x2019;s rights between Kinh and ethnic minority farmers. The former have generally higher income, higher education level and their children mostly help with light work in the farm while pursuing their educational objectives. The latter tend to have less access to decent jobs, smaller land size and are generally poorer. As a consequence, their children are more vulnerable to the risk of child labour and leaving school prematurely.To address child labour, the Rainforest Alliance is working together with global trader and local companies, and with strong involvement from the government, to achieve structural changes that will lead to the better livelihoods of rural families and their children in the target areas. Migrants will be a priority group, who will be targeted through monitoring, to understand better their situation and develop effective responses to their needs.The project is based on four main pillars and pathways of change:1.	 Improving families&apos; livelihoods by providing technical training in sustainable coffee production, as well as resources and material to pilot alternative income sources to diversify the local economy; 2.	Increasing school attendance and preventing school drop-out, with a particular focus on ethnic minority children; 3.	Guiding local and international coffee companies in improving their policies and sustainability practices, and sharing lessons learnt with the broader network of stakeholders; 4.	Coordinating the design of collaborative strategies and policies to promote ethnic minorities&#x2019; inclusion in government policies relating to poverty reduction, education for all and elimination of child labour.</narrative>
  </description>
